\n   *Verification.*  \n   - If a point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n-1\\), then it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n   - If \\(a=n\\), the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\); the point is \\((n,1)\\), and substituting \\(x=n\\) into the equation of \\(L_{\\text{sunny}}\\) gives \\(y=1\\), so \\((n,1)\\) lies on the sunny line.  \n   Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (2) shows that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge 2\\).  \n   The explicit constructions above realise \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n   Therefore the complete set of admissible values is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N_n=|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff it is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\n\\[\n\\text{vertical }(x=c),\\qquad\n\\text{horizontal }(y=c),\\qquad\n\\text{anti‑diagonal }(x+y=c).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reformulation as a vertex‑cover problem  \n\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) denote  \n\n* its **column** by \\(c_a=a\\);\n* its **row** by \\(r_b=b\\);\n* its **anti‑diagonal** by \\(d_{a+b}=a+b\\).\n\nIf a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of non‑sunny lines covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), then each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) belongs to one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_0=\\{x=i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal R_0=\\{y=j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal D_0=\\{x+y=k\\mid k=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C=\\{i\\mid x=i\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal R=\\{j\\mid y=j\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{k\\mid x+y=k\\in\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen a point \\((a,b)\\) is covered iff  \n\n\\[\na\\in\\mathcal C\\ \\text{or}\\ b\\in\\mathcal R\\ \\text{or}\\ a+b\\in\\mathcal D .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for \\(|\\mathcal C|+|\\mathcal D|\\)\n\nIgnore the rows \\(\\mathcal R\\) for the moment.  \nDefine a bipartite graph \\(G\\) with left vertex set \\(\\mathcal C_0\\) (columns) and right vertex set \\(\\mathcal D_0\\) (anti‑diagonals).  \nPlace an edge between \\(a\\in\\mathcal C_0\\) and \\(s\\in\\mathcal D_0\\) precisely when the point \\((a,s-a)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); equivalently, when \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and \\(a+1\\le s\\le n+1\\).\n\nThus the edge set of \\(G\\) is in one‑to‑one correspondence with \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nChoosing subsets \\(\\mathcal C\\subseteq\\mathcal C_0\\) and \\(\\mathcal D\\subseteq\\mathcal D_0\\) covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) exactly when \\(\\mathcal C\\cup\\mathcal D\\) is a **vertex cover** of \\(G\\).\n\n*Maximum matching.*  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) the edge \\((i,i+1)\\) (the point \\((i,1)\\)) belongs to \\(G\\).  \nThese \\(n-1\\) edges are pairwise disjoint, so they form a matching of size \\(n-1\\).  \nNo matching can be larger: the right‑hand vertex \\(2\\) is adjacent only to the left‑hand vertex \\(1\\); consequently any matching can contain at most one edge incident to \\(2\\), and there are only \\(n-1\\) right‑hand vertices altogether.  \nHence the **maximum matching size** of \\(G\\) is \\(\\nu(G)=n-1\\).\n\n*Minimum vertex cover.*  \nKönig’s theorem for bipartite graphs states \\(\\tau(G)=\\nu(G)\\), where \\(\\tau(G)\\) denotes the size of a minimum vertex cover.  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\tau(G)=n-1.\n\\]\n\nConsequently any pair \\((\\mathcal C,\\mathcal D)\\) satisfying (1) must obey  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal C|+|\\mathcal D|\\ge n-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Incorporating rows\n\nRows are additional vertices that are **not required** to cover the edges of \\(G\\).  \nIf a family \\((\\mathcal C,\\mathcal R,\\mathcal D)\\) satisfies (1), then certainly \\((\\mathcal C,\\mathcal D)\\) already covers every edge of \\(G\\); otherwise there would exist a point whose column and anti‑diagonal are both absent, contradicting (1).  \nThus (2) holds for any covering family, regardless of the presence of rows.  \n\nHence any covering by non‑sunny lines contains at least \\(n-1\\) lines, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\text{number of sunny lines }k\\le n-(n-1)=1. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) (because \\(b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \nThus \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nAll lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, so none is sunny.  \nTherefore the configuration satisfies the required covering and has \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Existence for \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\nand add the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{sunny}}:\\; y = 2\\bigl(x-n\\bigr)+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0\\), from undefined (vertical), and from \\(-1\\); hence the line is sunny.\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n*Case \\(a\\le n-1\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n\n*Case \\(a=n\\).*  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b=1\\); the point is \\((n,1)\\).  \nSubstituting \\(x=n\\) into the equation of \\(L_{\\text{sunny}}\\) gives \\(y=1\\), so \\((n,1)\\) belongs to the sunny line.  \n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final conclusion  \n\nInequality (3) shows that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge 2\\).  \nThe explicit constructions above realise \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nHence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\] ∎"
