ern suggests that any leftover set of points after using n-2 non-sunny lines will be of size at least 3 and arranged such that no pair can be covered by a sunny line. But we need to be sure.\n\nWe need to prove that at most one sunny line is possible. The solution attempted to prove that any covering must have at least n-1 non-sunny lines. We found a flaw in their proof but perhaps the statement is still true. Let's try to prove it ourselves.\n\nWe have n lines total, k sunny, n-k non-sunny. We need to show n-k ≥ n-1, i.e., k ≤ 1. So we need to show that any covering must have at least n-1 non-sunny lines. Equivalent to show that any covering with at most n-2 non-sunny lines is impossible.\n\nSuppose we have a covering with at most n-2 non-sunny lines. Then there are at least 2 sunny lines. Let's denote the sets of non-sunny lines: vertical lines X = {x = c_i}, horizontal lines Y = {y = r_j}, anti-diagonal lines Z = {x+y = d_k}. Let |X| + |Y| + |Z| ≤ n-2.\n\nGoal: Show that there exists a point (a,b) ∈ P_n not covered.\n\nWe can think of the covering condition: each point must satisfy a ∈ C or b ∈ R or a+b ∈ D, where C = set of column indices of vertical lines, R = set of row indices of horizontal lines, D = set of anti-diagonal sums of anti-diagonal lines.\n\nThus we have sets C,R,D with |C|+|R|+|D| ≤ n-2.\n\nWe need to show there exists (a,b) with a+b ≤ n+1 such that a ∉ C, b ∉ R, a+b ∉ D.\n\nThis is a combinatorial covering problem. Equivalent to: The complement sets C' = [1..n] \\ C, R' = [1..n] \\ R, D' = [2..n+1] \\ D. We need to find a ∈ C', b ∈ R', and a+b ∈ D'.\n\nIf we can find such a,b, then point (a,b) is uncovered. So we need to show that if |C|+|R|+|D| ≤ n-2, then there exists a,b ∈ positive integers with a ≤ n, b ≤ n, a+b ≤ n+1 such that a ∉ C, b ∉ R, a+b ∉ D. Actually we need a+b ∈ D'? Wait we need a+b not in D, so a+b ∈ D'.\n\nThus we need to find a ∈ C', b ∈ R', and a+b ∈ D'.\n\nWe have sizes: |C'| = n - |C|, |R'| = n - |R|, |D'| = (n) - |D| (since D runs from 2..n+1 inclusive, size n). So |D'| = n - |D|.\n\nWe have |C'| + |R'| + |D'| = 3n - (|C|+|R|+|D|). Since |C|+|R|+|D| ≤ n-2, we have |C'|+|R'|+|D'| ≥ 3n - (n-2) = 2n + 2.\n\nWe need to find a triple (a,b,s) with a ∈ C', b ∈ R', s ∈ D' such that s = a+b and a+b ≤ n+1. So we need to find a pair (a,b) ∈ C' × R' such that a+b ∈ D' and a+b ≤ n+1.\n\nAlternatively, we can think of the bipartite graph G between C' and R' where edges correspond to sums a+b ∈ D' and a+b ≤ n+1. Actually D' is the set of sums not covered by anti-diagonal lines. So we need to find an edge in the bipartite graph between C' and R' such that the sum a+b ∈ D' (i.e., not covered by anti-diagonal lines). If there is such an edge, then the point (a,b) is uncovered.\n\nThus we need to show that if |C|+|R|+|D| ≤ n-2, then there exists a ∈ C', b ∈ R' such that a+b ∈ D' and a+b ≤ n+1. This is a combinatorial existence problem.\n\nWe can try to prove by counting: The total number of possible pairs (a,b) with a ∈ C', b ∈ R' and a+b ≤ n+1 is something. The number of sums a+b that are in D is at most |D|. So if we have many pairs, perhaps one of them has sum not in D.\n\nAlternatively, consider the set S = {a+b | a ∈ C', b ∈ R', a+b ≤ n+1}. This set is a subset of [2, n+1] (since a,b ≥ 1). Its size is at most n (the size of D). If we can show that |S| > |D|, then there must be a sum in S not in D, i.e., in D'. So we need to show that the number of distinct sums from C' × R' (restricted to ≤ n+1) exceeds |D| = n - |D'|? Actually we need to show that there is at least one sum in S that is not in D, i.e., S \\ D ≠ ∅.\n\nWe have |D| = n - |D'|. So |D'| = n - |D|. We want to show that S cannot be a subset of D (i.e., S ⊆ D) if |C|+|R|+|D| ≤ n-2. Equivalent to show that |S| > |D|.\n\nWe need to bound |S| from below in terms of |C'| and |R'|. Since C' and R' are subsets of [1..n] of sizes c' = n - |C| and r' = n - |R|. So c' + r' = 2n - (|C|+|R|). Since |C|+|R|+|D| ≤ n-2, we have |C|+|R| ≤ n-2 - |D|. So c