D_{a+b}\\in\\mathcal L .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound on the number of non‑sunny lines\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nAny covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines contains at least \\(n-1\\)\nnon‑sunny lines.\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\).*\n\n*Base \\(n=3\\).*  \n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nEach of the three points has a distinct column, row and anti‑diagonal; any\nsingle line can contain at most two of them. Hence at least two of the three\nrequired lines must be non‑sunny, i.e. at least \\(3-1\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for \\(n-1\\) (\\(n\\ge4\\)).  \nConsider a covering \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) lines.\nThe point \\((n,1)\\in T_n\\) must be covered, so one of the three lines  \n\n\\[\nC_n\\;(x=n),\\qquad R_1\\;(y=1),\\qquad D_{n+1}\\;(x+y=n+1)\n\\]\n\nbelongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\). We treat the three possibilities separately.\n\n*Case 1: \\(C_n\\in\\mathcal L\\).*  \nDelete \\(C_n\\) and the single point \\((n,1)\\) it covers.\nThe remaining points are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-1,\\;1\\le b,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}=T_{n-1}.\n\\]\n\nThe other \\(n-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) still cover this copy of \\(T_{n-1}\\).\nBy the induction hypothesis at least \\( (n-1)-1 = n-2\\) of those are\nnon‑sunny. Adding the column \\(C_n\\) (which is non‑sunny) yields at least\n\\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines in total.\n\n*Case 2: \\(R_1\\in\\mathcal L\\).*  \nThe row \\(R_1\\) covers all points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).\nRemove these points; the rest satisfy \\(b\\ge2\\). Subtract \\(1\\) from the second\ncoordinate:\n\\[\n(a,b)\\mapsto (a,b-1).\n\\]\nThe image is precisely \\(T_{n-1}\\).  \nUnder this translation a column \\(x=c\\) remains a column, a row \\(y=d\\) with\n\\(d\\ge2\\) becomes the row \\(y'=d-1\\), and an anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) becomes\n\\(x+y=s-1\\). Hence the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) form a covering\nof \\(T_{n-1}\\). By induction at least \\(n-2\\) of them are non‑sunny; together\nwith the row \\(R_1\\) (non‑sunny) we obtain at least \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Case 3: \\(D_{n+1}\\in\\mathcal L\\).*  \nThe anti‑diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) consists of the points \\((a,n+1-a)\\) for\n\\(1\\le a\\le n\\). Removing them leaves all points with \\(a+b\\le n\\), i.e.\n\\(T_{n-1}\\). No coordinate shift is needed; a column, a row and an\nanti‑diagonal of the original configuration remain of the same type inside\n\\(T_{n-1}\\). Thus the other \\(n-1\\) lines cover \\(T_{n-1}\\), and by the\ninduction hypothesis at least \\(n-2\\) of them are non‑sunny. Adding the\nanti‑diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) (non‑sunny) gives at least \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nIn every case a covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) lines uses at least \\(n-1\\)\nnon‑sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\nFrom Lemma 1 we obtain the immediate corollary\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le 1}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Realising \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) (because \\(b\\ge1\\) and\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\)). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nAll lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, so none is sunny.  \nThus the configuration satisfies the covering condition with exactly\n\\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Realising \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\nand add the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{sunny}}:\\; y = 2\\bigl(x-n\\bigr)+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(2\\) is neither \\(0\\) (horizontal), nor undefined (vertical), nor\n\\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)); therefore \\(L_{\\text{sunny}}\\) is sunny.\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n* If a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n-1\\), then it lies on the\nvertical line \\(x=a\\) which is in the family \\(\\{L_i\\}\\).\n\n* If \\(a=n\\), the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\); the point is\n\\((n,1)\\). Substituting \\(x=n\\) into the equation of \\(L_{\\text{sunny}}\\)\ngives \\(y=1\\), so \\((n,1)\\) belongs to the sunny line.\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Completion of the proof\n\nLemma 1 yields the universal bound \\(k\\le1\\) for any admissible configuration.\nThe constructions of Sections 2 and 3 show that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are\nindeed achievable for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nConsequently the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required\nconfiguration exists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\] ∎"
