1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (non\u2011sunny) and add one sunny\n   line passing through the unique point \\((n,1)\\), e.g.  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{\\text{sunny}}:\\; y=2\\bigl(x-n\\bigr)+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus the line is sunny.\n   Every point with \\(a\\le n-1\\) lies on its column, and the point \\((n,1)\\)\n   lies on \\(L_{\\text{sunny}}\\).  Hence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered\n   and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-3\\) columns \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) (non\u2011sunny).  The six points\n   that remain are  \n\n   \\[\n   A=(n-2,1),\\;B=(n-2,2),\\;C=(n-2,3),\\;\n   D=(n-1,1),\\;E=(n-1,2),\\;F=(n,1).\n   \\]\n\n   Pair them as  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;A\\text{ and }E,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;B\\text{ and }F,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;C\\text{ and }D .\n   \\]\n\n   Their slopes are respectively \\(1,\\;-1/2,\\;-2\\); none equals \\(0,\\infty\\) or\n   \\(-1\\), so all three lines are sunny.  Together with the \\(n-3\\) columns they\n   form a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(T_n\\), and\n   exactly three of them are sunny.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 forces \\(k\\le3\\); the argument of step\u202f3 shows that \\(k=2\\) cannot\n   occur; constructions in step\u202f4 exhibit configurations for \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n   Therefore the complete set of admissible values is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\in\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}} .\n   \\] \u220e"
