n)\\);  \n   * anti‑diagonals \\(x+y=s\\;(2\\le s\\le n+1)\\).  \n\n   For a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we denote its three associated\n   non‑sunny lines by  \n\n   \\[\n   C_a:x=a,\\qquad R_b:y=b,\\qquad D_{a+b}:x+y=a+b .\n   \\]\n\n   A family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers the required set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   iff for every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) at least one of \\(C_a,R_b,D_{a+b}\\) belongs to\n   \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n2. **Lower bound on the number of non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(m\\) be the number of non‑sunny lines in a covering.  \n   **Lemma 1.**  For any covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines one has  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\ge n-3 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Choose \\(m\\) columns \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\) (any other choice of non‑sunny lines\n   can be replaced by columns without decreasing the number of points they\n   cover).  \n   These columns contain  \n\n   \\[\n   S(m)=\\sum_{c=1}^{m}(n+1-c)=m(n+1)-\\frac{m(m+1)}2\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   The remaining points are  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|-S(m)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-m(n+1)+\\frac{m(m+1)}2\n   =\\frac{(n+1)(n-2m)+m(m+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The other \\(n-m\\) lines are sunny; a sunny line can contain at most two\n   points of \\(T_n\\) (otherwise the line would be parallel to one of the three\n   forbidden directions).  Hence the sunny lines can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   2(n-m)\n   \\]\n\n   points.  Requiring (3) \\(\\le 2(n-m)\\) yields after elementary algebra  \n\n   \\[\n   m^2+(3-2n)m+(n^2-3n)\\le0 .\n   \\]\n\n   The discriminant of this quadratic equals \\(9\\); its roots are\n   \\(m=n-3\\) and \\(m=n\\).  Consequently the inequality holds exactly for\n   \\(n-3\\le m\\le n\\), proving (2). ∎\n\n   From (2) we obtain the **upper bound**\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le 3 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   Assume a covering with exactly two sunny lines, i.e. \\(m=n-2\\) non‑sunny lines.\n   By Lemma 1 we must have \\(n-2\\ge n-3\\), so the situation is possible only\n   when the non‑sunny lines are precisely the columns  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   (Any other choice would leave at least one of the columns \\(x=n-1\\) or\n   \\(x=n\\) uncovered, increasing the number of remaining points and making a\n   covering with only two sunny lines impossible.)\n\n   After removing the columns (5) the uncovered points are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,1) .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Any line through two of the points in (6) has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &(n-1,1)-(n-1,2):\\ \\text{vertical (slope }\\infty),\\\\\n   &(n-1,1)-(n,1):\\ \\text{horizontal (slope }0),\\\\\n   &(n-1,2)-(n,1):\\ \\text{slope }-1 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   All three slopes are forbidden, therefore **no sunny line can pass through\n   any two of the points in (6)**.  Consequently the three points cannot be\n   covered by only two sunny lines, contradicting the assumption.  Hence\n\n   \\[\n   k\\neq2 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Realising the admissible values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) vertical columns \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  Every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\)\n   satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), so it lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  All lines are\n   non‑sunny, giving \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) columns \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (non‑sunny) and add one sunny\n   line passing through the unique point \\((n,1)\\), e.g.  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{\\text{sunny}}:\\; y=2\\bigl(x-n\\bigr)+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus the line is sunny.\n   Every point with \\(a\\le n-1\\) lies on its column, and the point \\((n,1)\\)\n   lies on \\(L_{\\text{sunny}}\\).  Hence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered\n   and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-3\\) columns \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) (non‑sunny).  The six points\n   that remain are  \n\n   \\[\n   A=(n-2,1),\\;B=(n-2,2),\\;C=(n-2,3),\\;\n   D=(n-1,1),\\;E=(n-1,2),\\;F=(n,1).\n   \\]\n\n   Pair them as  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;A\\text{ and }E,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;B\\text{ and }F,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;C\\text{ and }D .\n   \\]\n\n   Their slopes are respectively \\(1,\\;-1/2,\\;-2\\); none equals \\(0,\\infty\\) or\n   \\(-1\\), so all three lines are sunny.  Together with the \\(n-3\\) columns they\n   form a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(T_n\\), and\n   exactly three of them are sunny.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 forces \\(k\\le3\\); the argument of step 3 shows that \\(k=2\\) cannot\n   occur; constructions in step 4 exhibit configurations for \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n   Therefore the complete set of admissible values is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\in\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}} .\n   \\] ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: p=3 ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Explicit verification for p=3 provided ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"\n    *   **Issue:** Justification Gap - The solution should explicitly state \"since p>3 is prime, it's not divisible by 3, so p≡1 or 2 