"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Re\u2011formulation of the problem.**  \n   The three forbidden directions give three families of *non\u2011sunny* lines:  \n   * vertical columns \\(C_a:\\;x=a\\;(1\\le a\\le n)\\);  \n   * horizontal rows \\(R_b:\\;y=b\\;(1\\le b\\le n)\\);  \n   * anti\u2011diagonals \\(D_s:\\;x+y=s\\;(2\\le s\\le n+1)\\).  \n\n   For a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we denote its three associated\n   non\u2011sunny lines by  \n\n   \\[\n   C_a,\\qquad R_b,\\qquad D_{a+b}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   A family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers the required set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   iff for every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) at least one of the three lines in (1) belongs\n   to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n2. **A reduction lemma.**  \n\n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   Let \\(m\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines in a covering of \\(T_n\\) by\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Then  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\ge n-3 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Choose the \\(m\\) columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_m\\).  (Any other choice of\n   non\u2011sunny lines can be replaced by columns without decreasing the number of\n   points they cover; this follows because every column, row or anti\u2011diagonal\n   contains the same or a larger number of points than any other non\u2011sunny line\n   with the same \u201cdistance\u2019\u2019 from the origin.)  \n\n   Column \\(C_c\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(T_n\\); therefore the\n   \\(m\\) chosen columns cover  \n\n   \\[\n   S(m)=\\sum_{c=1}^{m}(n+1-c)=m(n+1)-\\frac{m(m+1)}2\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   points.  The set of points still uncovered is  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{c=1}^{m}C_c\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge m+1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   After the translation \\(a'=a-m\\) the set (5) becomes exactly the triangle\n   \\(T_{n-m}\\).  Consequently the number of uncovered points equals  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_{n-m}|=\\frac{(n-m)(n-m+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The remaining \\(n-m\\) lines are all sunny.  A sunny line has slope different\n   from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it meets each side of the triangle\n   \\(T_{n-m}\\) in at most one lattice point.  Hence a sunny line can contain at\n   most three points of \\(T_{n-m}\\) (one on each side).  Consequently the\n   \\(n-m\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(3(n-m)\\) points of \\(T_{n-m}\\).\n\n   For \\(n-m\\ge4\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_{n-m}|=\\frac{(n-m)(n-m+1)}2>3(n-m),\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.  Therefore we must have \\(n-m\\le3\\), i.e. \\(m\\ge n-3\\).\n   \u220e\n\n   Lemma\u202f1 yields the **upper bound**\n\n   \\[\n   k=n-m\\le3 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n\n   Assume a covering with exactly two sunny lines, i.e. \\(m=n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n   By Lemma\u202f1 we must have \\(n-2\\ge n-3\\), so the non\u2011sunny lines are precisely\n   the columns  \n\n   \\[\n   C_1,\\dots ,C_{\\,n-2}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   After removing the columns (8) the uncovered points are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,1) .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Any line through two of the points in (9) has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &(n-1,1)-(n-1,2):\\ \\text{vertical }(\\infty),\\\\\n   &(n-1,1)-(n,1):\\ \\text{horizontal }(0),\\\\\n   &(n-1,2)-(n,1):\\ \\text{slope }-1 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   All three slopes are forbidden, therefore **no sunny line can pass through any\n   two of the points in (9)**.  Consequently the three points cannot be covered\n   by only two sunny lines, a contradiction.  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\neq2 .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Explicit constructions for the admissible values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) vertical columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_n\\).  Every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has\n   \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), so it lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  All lines are non\u2011sunny, giving\n   \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{\\,n-1}\\) (non\u2011sunny) and add one sunny\n   line passing through the unique point \\((n,1)\\), e.g.  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{\\text{sunny}}:\\; y=2\\bigl(x-n\\bigr)+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus the line is sunny.\n   Every point with \\(a\\le n-1\\) lies on its column, and the point \\((n,1)\\) lies\n   on \\(L_{\\text{sunny}}\\).  Hence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly\n   one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-3\\) columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{\\,n-3}\\) (non\u2011sunny).  The six points\n   that remain are  \n\n   \\[\n   A=(n-2,1),\\;B=(n-2,2),\\;C=(n-2,3),\\;\n   D=(n-1,1),\\;E=(n-1,2),\\;F=(n,1).\n   \\]\n\n   Pair them as  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;A\\text{ and }E,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;B\\text{ and }F,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;C\\text{ and }D .\n   \\]\n\n   Their slopes are respectively \\(1,\\;-1/2,\\;-2\\); none equals \\(0,\\infty\\) or\n   \\(-1\\), so all three lines are sunny.  Together with the \\(n-3\\) columns they\n   form a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(T_n\\), and\n   exactly three of them are sunny.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 forces \\(k\\le3\\); the argument of step\u202f3 shows that \\(k=2\\) cannot\n   occur; constructions in step\u202f4 exhibit configurations for \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n   Therefore the complete set of admissible values is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\in\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}} .\n   \\] \u220e"
